Double Write Buffer (DWB) Design and Implementation Notes
==========================================================

Background and Goals
--------------------

PostgreSQL's Full Page Writes (FPW) protect against torn pages: after a crash,
if only part of an 8 kB data page reached disk, the full-page image recorded in
WAL restores it.  The cost is WAL write amplification — every page's first
modification after a checkpoint writes the entire 8 kB to WAL.

The Double Write Buffer (DWB) is an alternative mechanism, analogous to
InnoDB's doublewrite buffer.  Pages are written to a dedicated DWB file before
being written to the real data files.  If a crash occurs during a data-file
write, recovery reads the page from the DWB instead of relying on WAL FPW.

This reduces WAL write amplification while preserving torn-page protection.


GUC Control
-----------

  io_torn_pages_protection = off | full_pages | double_writes

  off           — no protection (dangerous)
  full_pages    — Full Page Writes to WAL (default PostgreSQL behaviour)
  double_writes — DWB mode (this feature)

  double_write_buffer_size = <MB>   (default 64 MB, range 16–1024 MB)

DWB is not a complete replacement for FPW in all paths:
  - When the DWB ring buffer is full the write falls back to a WAL FPI.
  - During online backup (runningBackups > 0) FPW is used regardless.


File Layout
-----------

  $PGDATA/pg_dwbuf/
  ├── dwbuf_000    (segment 0, up to DWBUF_SLOTS_PER_FILE = 4096 slots)
  ├── dwbuf_001    (segment 1)
  ...
  └── dwbuf_NNN    (up to DWBUF_MAX_FILES = 16 segment files)

Total capacity: double_write_buffer_size, spread across segment files.

Each file:

  [ File header, padded to DWBUF_FILE_HEADER_SIZE (4096 B) ]
  [ Slot 0: DWBufPageSlot header + 8 kB page data, aligned to PG_IO_ALIGN_SIZE ]
  [ Slot 1 ]
  ...
  [ Slot N-1 ]

DWBufPageSlot (on-disk slot header):

  pg_crc32c      crc;       -- CRC over header (excl. crc field) + page data
  RelFileLocator rlocator;  -- identifies the relation
  ForkNumber     forknum;
  BlockNumber    blkno;
  XLogRecPtr     lsn;       -- page LSN at write time
  uint32         slot_id;   -- monotonically increasing global slot number
  uint16         flags;     -- DWBUF_SLOT_VALID = 0x0001
  uint16         checksum;  -- page checksum

DWBufFileHeader (first DWBUF_FILE_HEADER_SIZE bytes of each file):

  uint32    magic;           -- 0x44574246 ("DWBF")
  uint32    version;         -- DWBUF_VERSION = 1
  uint32    blcksz;          -- must match BLCKSZ
  uint32    slots_per_file;
  pg_crc32c crc;             -- CRC over preceding fields


Shared Memory Control Structure (DWBufCtlData)
----------------------------------------------

All atomics; no LWLock required for the normal write path.

  write_pos            -- next slot number to allocate (monotonically increasing)
  flush_pos            -- write_pos value at last DWBufFlush()
  active_writers       -- count of writers between fetch_add(write_pos) and
                           completion of pg_pwrite()
  resetting            -- quiesce flag; 1 while PreCheckpoint or PostCheckpoint
                           is draining active writers (see below)
  checkpoint_start_pos -- write_pos snapshot taken by DWBufPreCheckpoint after
                           quiescing; used to detect ring-buffer wrap-around
  file_write_gen[]     -- per-file: incremented by each DWBufWritePage
  file_sync_gen[]      -- per-file: updated to file_write_gen after each fsync
  file_syncing[]       -- per-file CAS lock: 1 while an fsync is in progress
  overflow_count       -- cumulative count of ring-buffer overflow FPI fallbacks
  num_slots            -- total ring-buffer capacity
  num_files            -- number of segment files
  slots_per_file


Normal Write Path (DWBufWritePage)
-----------------------------------

1. Ensure files are open in this process (PID-tracked, re-opened after fork).

2. Quiesce coordination:
     loop:
       spin-wait while resetting == 1   [CHECK_FOR_INTERRUPTS() on each iter]
       atomic_fetch_add(active_writers, 1)
       if resetting still 0: break      -- successfully registered
       atomic_fetch_sub(active_writers, 1)  -- lost race, retry

3. Allocate slot: pos = atomic_fetch_add(write_pos, 1)

4. Ring-buffer overflow check:
     ckpt_start = checkpoint_start_pos
     if pos - ckpt_start >= num_slots:
         atomic_fetch_sub(active_writers, 1)
         atomic_fetch_add(overflow_count, 1)
         emit DEBUG2 log
         return -1   -- caller falls back to FPI

5. Compute physical location:
     slot_pos = pos % num_slots
     file_idx = slot_pos / slots_per_file
     slot_idx = slot_pos % slots_per_file
     offset   = DWBUF_FILE_HEADER_SIZE + slot_idx * DWBUF_SLOT_SIZE

6. Build slot header in process-local buffer; compute CRC32C over header
   (excluding crc field) + page data; write CRC into header.

7. pg_pwrite() DWBUF_SLOT_SIZE bytes to DWB file; track IO via pgstat.

8. atomic_fetch_add(file_write_gen[file_idx], 1)  -- record write generation
   Return write_gen to caller via out-parameter.

9. atomic_fetch_sub(active_writers, 1)

10. Return file_idx.


Batch fsync Optimisation (DWBufFlushFile)
------------------------------------------

Non-checkpoint callers (FlushBuffer in bufmgr.c) call DWBufFlushFile(file_idx,
write_gen) after DWBufWritePage returns.  The optimisation:

  fast path: if file_sync_gen[file_idx] >= write_gen → return (no syscall)

  slow path:
    CAS file_syncing[file_idx]: 0 → 1
      success: re-check sync_gen (another syncer may have just finished);
               pg_fsync(); advance file_sync_gen to current file_write_gen;
               clear file_syncing.
      failure: spin-wait for file_syncing == 0  [CHECK_FOR_INTERRUPTS() on
               each iteration]; re-check sync_gen; if still insufficient retry.

A single fsync can satisfy many concurrent writers targeting the same file.


Checkpoint Integration
-----------------------

DWBufPreCheckpoint() — called at the start of CheckpointGuts():

  The function must guarantee that DWBufFlush() covers every slot allocated
  before this point.  A naive snapshot-then-fsync has a race: a writer may
  have incremented write_pos but not yet issued pg_pwrite() when we take the
  snapshot; the subsequent fsync would miss that slot.

  Fix: reuse the resetting flag to quiesce all writers first.

    1. resetting = 1  (blocks new writers)
    2. spin-wait active_writers == 0  (all in-flight pwrite()s complete)
    3. DWBufFlush()  (fsync covers every allocated slot)
    4. checkpoint_start_pos = write_pos  (ring-buffer boundary for new cycle)
    5. resetting = 0  (writers resume)

  Note: CHECK_FOR_INTERRUPTS is intentionally absent from step 2 because we
  hold resetting == 1.  An error exit would leave resetting permanently set,
  blocking all future DWB writes.  Step 2 is bounded by pwrite latency only
  and completes quickly.

DWBufPostCheckpoint() — called after CheckpointGuts() completes:

  1. resetting = 1
  2. spin-wait active_writers == 0
  3. write_pos = 0; flush_pos = 0; checkpoint_start_pos = 0
  4. resetting = 0

  Resetting checkpoint_start_pos to 0 is essential: without it, the next
  DWBufWritePage would compute (pos - old_checkpoint_start_pos) with uint64
  underflow and every write would overflow until the next PreCheckpoint.

BufferSync() Phase 1 (batch DWB write during checkpoint):

  Before Phase 2 (the normal dirty-page flush loop), the checkpointer iterates
  all checkpoint-needed buffers and writes them to DWB in bulk, then calls
  DWBufFlush() once.  This replaces the per-page DWBufWritePage + DWBufFlushFile
  that FlushBuffer would otherwise do, collapsing N fsyncs into one per segment
  file.

  For pages that return -1 from DWBufWritePage (ring buffer full), Phase 1
  falls back to log_newpage() + XLogFlush() — the standard WAL FPI path.
  The per-file fallback count is logged at LOG level when > 0.

  After Phase 1 sets DWBufCheckpointWritesDone(true), FlushBuffer skips its
  own DWBufWritePage call for checkpoint-needed pages.  The flag is cleared
  after BufferSync() finishes.


Crash Recovery (DWBufRecoveryInit / DWBufRecoverPage)
------------------------------------------------------

Called from StartupXLOG() before WAL replay begins.

DWBufRecoveryInit():
  1. Create in-memory hash table keyed by (rlocator, forknum, blkno).
  2. Scan all pg_dwbuf/dwbuf_NNN files:
       a. Validate file header magic + CRC; skip on mismatch.
       b. For each slot: check DWBUF_SLOT_VALID flag; verify CRC.
       c. Insert valid slot into hash table.  If a later slot has a higher
          LSN (or equal LSN but higher slot_id), it replaces the earlier one.
       d. Page data is copied into TopMemoryContext so recovery does not
          need to re-read DWB files (which may be overwritten during replay).

DWBufRecoverPage(rlocator, forknum, blkno, page_buf):
  Look up the hash table.  If found and page_data != NULL, memcpy and return
  true.  Called by buffer_readv_complete_one() when PageIsVerified() fails.


Fork Safety
-----------

File descriptors are per-process and must not be inherited across fork().

  DWBufFds[DWBUF_MAX_FILES]  — per-process FDs, initialised to -1
  DWBufFilesOpenedPid         — PID that opened the FDs

DWBufOpenFiles() checks getpid() == DWBufFilesOpenedPid.  If the PID differs
(child process after fork), it calls DWBufClose() to close inherited FDs before
reopening.  This prevents EBADF or silent cross-process sharing.


IO Statistics
-------------

DWB IO is tracked through the pgstat_io framework:

  IOOBJECT_DWBUF + IOCONTEXT_NORMAL
  IOOP_WRITE  — each DWBufWritePage pwrite()
  IOOP_FSYNC  — each pg_fsync() in DWBufFlush() or DWBufFlushFile()


Known Limitations and Future Work
----------------------------------

Memory usage during recovery:
  Page data is stored in TopMemoryContext (one BLCKSZ copy per valid slot).
  With double_write_buffer_size = 1024 MB this can reach ~1 GB.  A future
  improvement is streaming recovery: store only slot metadata and re-read
  pages on demand.

Version migration:
  DWBufFileHeader.version = 1.  There is currently no upgrade path for future
  format changes, and pg_upgrade does not clear pg_dwbuf.  A future version
  should add a min_version field and clear pg_dwbuf on version mismatch,
  following the pg_wal precedent.

Overflow monitoring:
  DWBufCtl->overflow_count records cumulative ring-buffer overflows.  A non-zero
  and growing value indicates double_write_buffer_size is too small.  Future work
  could expose this counter through pg_stat_io or a dedicated view.
